{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-102804",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{3x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x})-({u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x})-6u{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}({u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}+9u-{u}^{′}-3u-6u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}({u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{3x}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-5x}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5}{e}^{-5x}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{3x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5}{e}^{-5x}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{-5x}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$",
